title: Big scenes in the editor
intro: Twenty thousand things playing in the editor, smoothly, recorded for rewind ten times a second, with live panels.
project: BunnyMark | Big Scene

chapter: Twenty thousand bunnies | Played right in the editor
select: BunnyMark
explain: field:BunnyMark.HoldCount | Hold Count | BunnyMark can put a number of bunnies in straight away. Twenty thousand makes a big scene: each one is an entity with a sprite and a script.
set: BunnyMark.HoldCount = 20000
set: BunnyMark.HoldSeconds = 4
play
wait: 2
explain: panel:Hierarchy | All twenty thousand | The Hierarchy fills at once. It only makes the rows you can see, and it catches up with the game ten times a second rather than every frame.
select: Bunny
explain: panel:Inspector | Live values | Pick one, and the Inspector shows its values changing as it bounces.
explain: game | Smooth | The game keeps its frame rate while it records for rewind. Between two steps it copies the bunnies out, which is quick, and it works out what changed on another thread.
wait: 3
explain: game | Timed | BunnyMark times every frame while it holds them, and shows the frame rate, its one percent low and the garbage collections along the bottom.

chapter: Rewind a big scene | The recording keeps up
pause
explain: rewind | The Rewind bar | Griff has been recording all along, ten times a second, even with twenty thousand bunnies. Each snapshot keeps just the bytes that changed since the one before, so a bunny takes a few dozen bytes.
rewind: 0.2
explain: game | Back in time | The bunnies are back where they were. Only a scene too big for the recording to fit in memory is recorded less often.
pause
wait: 2
stop
say: Every tenth snapshot is kept whole, so rewinding never has far to go. And a script can check how it all goes: BunnyBench, in the tools folder, times the recording too.
